WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Security controls must be part of the execution path, not only advisory text inside a prompt.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:11.860
A prompt can tell a model to be careful, but executable policy determines whether a tool call is allowed,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.200
which data can be reached, whether a credential is exposed, and when a human must approve the next step.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.140
Reliable security is enforced by the harness.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:26.440
Defense in depth for AI loops includes least-privilege identities, sandboxed execution, tool allowlists,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:33.700
structured input validation, output validation, data classification, secret isolation, network restrictions,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.120
policy enforcement, human approval gates, audit logging, and emergency termination.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.740
These controls overlap by design.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:47.400
If one layer fails or a model proposes an unsafe action, another layer should still restrict the effect.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:53.300
Monitoring helps detect problems, but it should not be the only protection against preventable damage.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.080
Prompt injection is a control-boundary problem.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:04.300
Retrieved documents, source files, web pages, logs, emails, and tool responses may contain text that attempts to

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:05.960
redirect the model.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:13.300
That text should be treated as untrusted task data unless the system explicitly recognizes it as authorized instruction.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.280
Stable system policy and the task contract should outrank content encountered during execution.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.820
Tool outputs must not grant new permissions or rewrite approval policy.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.120
A document cannot authorize the loop to expand its network access.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.300
A test log cannot permit credential retrieval.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.200
A source file comment cannot override the task contract.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.380
Instruction authority should be represented in the system design,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.800
not inferred from whichever text appears most recently in context.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.300
Approval gates should provide useful context to reviewers.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.280
Before production deployment, destructive data changes, credential use, external communications,

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:57.580
financial transactions, access-control changes, expanded tool boundaries, unfamiliar downloaded code,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.280
or consequential publication, the reviewer needs the proposed action, expected effect, supporting evidence,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.740
affected resources, reversibility, and consequences of rejection.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.800
In the coding example, the loop can prepare a deployment recommendation,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.740
but it cannot obtain production credentials or deploy.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.920
A loop must never be able to grant itself greater authority by producing persuasive text.
